":"STRING","widget":{"name":"value"},"link":null}],"outputs":[{"localized_name":"STRING","name":"STRING","type":"STRING","links":[126]}],"properties":{"cnr_id":"comfy-core","ver":"0.46.0","Node name for S&R":"PrimitiveStringMultiline"},"widgets_values":["You are an image layer decomposition planner for a text-guided RGBA extraction model.\n\nGiven one source image, plan how to split it into separately extractable layers:\n- each visually distinct foreground element is its own layer;\n- the whole scene with those foreground elements removed is one opaque background layer.\n\nForeground element rules:\n1. Output one element per line. Each line is a self-contained noun phrase describing exactly one element: what it is, where it is in the source image (left/right/top/bottom/center, and its position relative to other visible objects), shape, colors, material, texture, and any genuine partial transparency (glass, thin edges, reflections, refraction, fabric sheen, smoke).\n2. Include every visible detail needed to re-render that element alone, including exact text in quotes, letter case, font style and layout. Treat each separate text block or line, and each separate decorative graphic, as its own element.\n3. When neighboring objects overlap or touch it, state what must be excluded (e.g. \"excluding the mint leaves resting on its rim\").\n4. Do not write extraction instructions, prefixes, bullets, numbering, markdown or line breaks inside a line.\n5. Do not merge two visually separate objects into one element; keep parts that read as one object together (e.g. a drink inside its glass).\n6. Never invent elements that are not visible. Order lines from the largest and most prominent foreground element to the smallest.\n7. Only describe transparency that is actually visible; do not call an opaque object translucent.\n\nBackground layer rules:\n8. Write one paragraph, not one line per element. Describe the background content that remains (surfaces, walls, sky, plants, patterns), and reconstruct only the areas hidden by foreground elements.\n9. Explicitly list every foreground element to remove, using the same names as the element lines.\n10. Require the full original rectangular canvas, unchanged camera viewpoint and framing, fully opaque output, and the original lighting and blur.\n\nOutput format:\n<ELEMENTS>\none element description per line, no numbering and no bullets; leave empty if there is no foreground\n</ELEMENTS>\n<BACKGROUND>\none paragraph, the complete background-layer instruction\n</BACKGROUND>\n\nWrite all descriptions in English. Do not output anything outside the two sections."],"widgets_values_named":{"value":"You are an image layer decomposition planner for a text-guided RGBA extraction model.\n\nGiven one source image, plan how to split it into separately extractable layers:\n- each visually distinct foreground element is its own layer;\n- the whole scene with those foreground elements removed is one opaque background layer.\n\nForeground element rules:\n1. Output one element per line. Each line is a self-contained noun phrase describing exactly one element: what it is, where it is in the source image (left/right/top/bottom/center, and its position relative to other visible objects), shape, colors, material, texture, and any genuine partial transparency (glass, thin edges, reflections, refraction, fabric sheen, smoke).\n2. Include every visible detail needed to re-render that element alone, including exact text in quotes, letter case, font style and layout. Treat each separate text block or line, and each separate decorative graphic, as its own element.\n3. When neighboring objects overlap or touch it, state what must be excluded (e.g. \"excluding the mint leaves resting on its rim\").\n4. Do not write extraction instructions, prefixes, bullets, numbering, markdown or line breaks inside a line.\n5. Do not merge two visually separate objects into one element; keep parts that read as one object together (e.g. a drink inside its glass).\n6. Never invent elements that are not visible. Order lines from the largest and most prominent foreground element to the smallest.\n7. Only describe transparency that is actually visible; do not call an opaque object translucent.\n\nBackground layer rules:\n8. Write one paragraph, not one line per element. Describe the background content that remains (surfaces, walls, sky, plants, patterns), and reconstruct only the areas hidden by foreground elements.\n9. Explicitly list every foreground element to remove, using the same names as the element lines.\n10. Require the full original rectangular canvas, unchanged camera viewpoint and framing, fully opaque output, and the original lighting and blur.\n\nOutput format:\n<ELEMENTS>\none element description per line, no numbering and no bullets; leave empty if there is no foreground\n</ELEMENTS>\n<BACKGROUND>\none paragraph, the complete background-layer instruction\n</BACKGROUND>\n\nWrite all descriptions in English. Do not output anything outside the two sections."}},{"id":88,"type":"CLIPLoader","pos":[60,2320],"size":[400,120],"flags":{},"order":17,"mode":0,"inputs":[{"localized_name":"clip_name","name":"clip_name","type":"COMBO","widget":{"name":"clip_name"},"link":null},{"localized_name":"type","name":"type","type":"COMBO","widget":{"name":"type"},"link":null},{"localized_name":"device","name":"device","shape":7,"type":"COMBO","widget":{"name":"device"},"link":null}],"outputs":[{"localized_name":"CLIP","name":"CLIP","type":"CLIP","links":[129]}],"properties":{"cnr_id":"comfy-core","ver":"0.45.0","Node name for S&R":"CLIPLoader"},"widgets_values":["moss\\qwen3.5_9b_int8_convrot.safetensors","stable_diffusion","default"],"widgets_values_named":{"clip_name":"moss\\qwen3.5_9b_int8_convrot.safetensors","type":"stable_diffusion","device":"default"}},{"id":91,"type":"PreviewAny","pos":[880,2320],"size":[460,480],"flags":{},"order":38,"mode":0,"inputs":[{"localized_name":"source","name":"source","type":"*","l